.\" find.1 - walk a file hierarchy
.TH FIND 1 "March 2026" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
find \- walk a file hierarchy evaluating a Boolean expression
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B find
.RB [ \-H | \-L | \-P ]
.RB [ \-E ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-x ]
.RB [ \-X ]
.RB [ \-D
.IR debugopts ]
.RB [ \-O\c
.IR level ]
.RB [ \-regextype
.IR type ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-files0\-from
.IR file ]
.RI [ path... ]
.RI [ expression ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B find
recursively descends the directory tree for each
.I path
listed, evaluating a Boolean
.I expression
for each file encountered.
If no path is given, the current directory
.Pq Sq \&.
is used.
If no expression is given,
.B \-print
is implied.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-H
Dereference symbolic links on the command line only.
.TP
.B \-L
Always follow symbolic links.
.TP
.B \-P
Never follow symbolic links (default).
.TP
.B \-E
Use extended regular expressions (ERE) for
.B \-regex
and
.BR \-iregex .
.TP
.B \-s
Sort directory entries lexicographically (BSD).
.TP
.B \-x
Do not descend into directories on other filesystems.
.TP
.BI \-f " path"
Explicitly specify a starting-point path (FreeBSD).
.TP
.B \-X
Modify output to be safe for use with
.BR xargs (1)
(OpenBSD).
.TP
.BI \-D " debugopts"
Print diagnostic information.
.I debugopts
is a comma-separated list of:
.BR tree " (expression tree),"
.BR stat " (trace stat calls),"
.BR opt " (optimizer actions),"
.BR rates " (predicate success rates),"
.BR exec " (trace exec calls),"
.BR all " (all of the above),"
.BR help " (list available flags)."
(GNU).
.TP
.BI \-O level
Set the optimization level (0\(en3).
Level 1 reorders filename tests before stat-requiring tests.
Level 2 reorders all pure tests before actions.
(GNU).
.TP
.BI \-regextype " type"
Set the regex flavor for
.B \-regex
and
.BR \-iregex .
Valid types:
.BR posix-basic ,
.BR posix-extended ,
.BR sed ,
.BR egrep ,
.BR posix-egrep .
(GNU).
.TP
.BI \-files0\-from " file"
Read starting-point paths from
.IR file ,
separated by NUL characters.
Use
.Sq \-
for standard input.
Cannot be combined with path arguments on the command line.
(GNU).
.SH PRIMARIES
.SS Tests
.TP
.BI \-name " pattern"
True if the basename matches the shell glob
.IR pattern .
.TP
.BI \-iname " pattern"
Like
.B \-name
but case-insensitive.
.TP
.BI \-path " pattern"
True if the full pathname matches
.IR pattern .
.TP
.BI \-type " c"
True if the file is of type
.IR c :
.BR f " (regular),"
.BR d " (directory),"
.BR l " (symlink),"
.BR c " (character),"
.BR b " (block),"
.BR p " (FIFO),"
.BR s " (socket)."
.TP
.BI \-perm " mode"
True if file permission bits match
.IR mode .
Prefix with
.B \-
for all-bits-set, or
.B /
for any-bit-set.
.TP
.BI \-user " name"
True if the file belongs to user
.IR name .
.TP
.BI \-group " name"
True if the file belongs to group
.IR name .
.TP
.BI \-size " n[ckMG]"
True if the file uses
.I n
units of space.
.TP
.BI \-newer " file"
True if the file was modified more recently than
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-newerXY " reference"
True if timestamp
.I X
of the file is newer than timestamp
.I Y
of
.IR reference .
.I X
and
.I Y
are one of
.BR a " (atime),"
.BR c " (ctime),"
.BR m " (mtime),"
or
.BR t " (time string, Y only)."
.TP
.B \-empty
True if the file is a regular file of size 0 or an empty directory.
.TP
.BI \-regex " pattern"
True if the full pathname matches the regular expression
.IR pattern .
.TP
.B \-readable
True if the file is readable by the current user (GNU).
.TP
.B \-writable
True if the file is writable by the current user (GNU).
.TP
.B \-executable
True if the file is executable by the current user (GNU).
.TP
.BI \-xtype " c"
Like
.B \-type
but tests the opposite stat (GNU).
.TP
.BI \-samefile " file"
True if the file has the same inode and device as
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-ilname " pattern"
True if the symlink target matches
.I pattern
case-insensitively (GNU).
.SS Actions
.TP
.B \-print
Print the pathname followed by a newline.
.TP
.B \-print0
Print the pathname followed by a NUL character.
.TP
.B \-ls
Print in
.BR ls (1)
format.
.TP
.BI \-printf " format"
Print according to
.IR format .
Supports
.BR %p ", " %f ", " %h ", " %s ", " %i ", " %d ", " %m ", " %u ", " %g ", " %T .
.TP
.BI \-exec " command" " {} ;"
Execute
.I command
for each matched file.
.TP
.BI \-exec " command" " {} +"
Like
.B \-exec
but batches pathnames.
.TP
.BI \-execdir " command" " {} ;"
Like
.B \-exec
but runs from the file's directory.
.TP
.B \-delete
Delete matched files. Implies
.BR \-depth .
.TP
.B \-prune
Do not descend into the current directory.
.TP
.B \-quit
Exit immediately.
.TP
.BI \-fprint " file"
Write pathnames to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-fprint0 " file"
Write NUL-terminated pathnames to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-fls " file"
Write ls-style output to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-fprintf " file" " format"
Write formatted output to
.IR file .
.SS Operators
.TP
.BR ( " " )
Grouping.
.TP
.BR ! " / " \-not
Negation.
.TP
.B \-a " / " \-and
Logical AND (default between adjacent primaries).
.TP
.B \-o " / " \-or
Logical OR.
.SS Global Modifiers
.TP
.B \-depth " / " \-d
Process directory contents before the directory itself.
.TP
.B \-xdev " / " \-mount
Do not descend into directories on other filesystems.
.TP
.BI \-maxdepth " n"
Descend at most
.I n
levels.
.TP
.BI \-mindepth " n"
Do not evaluate expression at levels less than
.IR n .
.TP
.B \-follow
Follow symbolic links (equivalent to
.BR \-L ).
.TP
.B \-daystart
Measure times from the beginning of today (GNU).
.TP
.BI \-fstype " type"
True if the file is on a filesystem of the given type (e.g.,
.BR ext2 ", " tmpfs ", " nfs ).
.TP
.B \-ignore_readdir_race
Suppress errors from files that disappear between readdir and stat (GNU).
.TP
.B \-noignore_readdir_race
Undo a previous
.B \-ignore_readdir_race
(GNU).
.TP
.B \-noleaf
Silently accepted for compatibility (GNU).
.TP
.BR \-warn " / " \-nowarn
Silently accepted for compatibility (GNU).
.TP
.B \-printx
Print pathnames with special characters backslash-escaped (NetBSD).
.TP
.BI \-ok " command" " {} ;"
Like
.B \-exec
but prompts the user before each execution.
.TP
.BI \-okdir " command" " {} ;"
Like
.B \-ok
but runs from the file's directory.
.TP
.B \-rm
Alias for
.B \-delete
(NetBSD).
.SH DIALECT PRECEDENCE
When BSD and GNU semantics conflict, FreeBSD behavior is the default.
GNU extensions are accepted but do not override BSD semantics.
.PP
Regex defaults to BRE; use
.B \-E
for ERE.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
.SS Regex
The default regex flavor is POSIX BRE (FreeBSD default).
Use
.B \-E
to switch to ERE, or
.B \-regextype
to select a specific flavor.
GNU Emacs regex is not supported.
.SS Permission Matching
.B \-perm /mode
(any\-bit match) and
.B \-perm +mode
(legacy GNU alias) are both accepted.
.SS Timestamps
.BI \-newerXY " ref"
uses the GNU two\-token form.
The FreeBSD three\-token form
.Pq Fl newer Ar XY Ar file
is not supported.
.SS Destructive Actions
.B \-delete
combined with
.B \-L
(follow mode) is rejected at startup.
Silently deleting symlink referents is a safety hazard.
.PP
.B \-delete
implies
.B \-depth
(post\-order traversal); placement in the expression matters.
.SS Batching
.BI \-exec " command" " {} +"
batches pathnames using both an ARG_MAX/2 byte\-size threshold
and a 5000\-pathname cap (FreeBSD limit).
.SS Feature Matrix
.TS
l l l.
Feature	Origin	Status
_
\-name, \-type, \-perm	POSIX	Supported
\-exec {} +, \-ok	POSIX	Supported
\-iname	POSIX Issue 8	Supported
\-H, \-L, \-P	POSIX	Supported
\-E, \-s, \-f, \-d	FreeBSD	Supported
\-empty, \-delete, \-execdir	BSD	Supported
\-ls, \-print0, \-regex	BSD	Supported
\-samefile, \-fstype	BSD	Supported
\-X, \-h	OpenBSD	Supported
\-printx, \-quit, \-rm	NetBSD	Supported
\-D, \-O, \-regextype	GNU	Supported
\-files0\-from, \-daystart	GNU	Supported
\-readable, \-writable	GNU	Supported
\-printf, \-fprintf, \-fls	GNU	Supported
\-newerXY, \-xtype, \-ilname	GNU	Supported
\-B* birthtime	FreeBSD	Unsupported
\-acl, \-flags, \-sparse	FreeBSD	Unsupported
\-exit, \-since	NetBSD	Unsupported
\-context	GNU/SELinux	Unsupported
.TE
.SH EXAMPLES
Search for C source files:
.RS
.nf
find . \-name "*.c" \-type f
.fi
.RE
.PP
Delete empty files safely:
.RS
.nf
find . \-empty \-type f \-delete
.fi
.RE
.PP
Safe filename handling with
.BR xargs (1):
.RS
.nf
find . \-type f \-print0 | xargs \-0 ls \-l
.fi
.RE
.PP
Find files modified in the last 24 hours:
.RS
.nf
find . \-mtime \-1
.fi
.RE
.SH SECURITY
.B \-execdir
rejects execution when the PATH environment variable contains
.Sq \&.
or an empty component.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B find
exits 0 if all files are processed successfully, and >0 if errors occur.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ls (1),
.BR xargs (1),
.BR stat (2),
.BR fnmatch (3),
.BR regex (3)
.SH STANDARDS
Conforms to IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (POSIX.1-2024).
FreeBSD, OpenBSD, NetBSD, and GNU findutils extensions supported.
